{
  "description": "Cross-binding pin for the EXACT TREE a reaction system lowers to, in derive_odes and in flatten (esm-libraries-spec §4.6.2). Every binding must produce these expressions, compared as JSON with integer and non-integer numbers kept apart. Hand-written: the expected trees follow from the §4.6.2 rules applied to each document, and were cross-checked against all five bindings; there is no generator and no oracle binding.",
  "why": "Derived documents copy flattened terms verbatim, so two bindings that lower the same reaction to different trees write different bytes. Before §4.6.2 they did: Julia wrote a loss as a unary minus and other coefficients as floats (`-2.0`), Rust as `-1.0 * rate`, Python, Go and TypeScript's flatten nested every `*` and `+` two operands at a time and dropped a literal `1` rate, and TypeScript's derive_odes used a unary minus and emitted `D(X, t) = 0` for a species in no reaction.",
  "how_to_drive": "For each case load `document`. (1) derive_odes on the reaction system named `system`: its equation list, in order, serialized with the binding's own expression writer, must equal `derive_odes` (each `{lhs, rhs}`). (2) flatten the document. Take, in order, the flattened equations that carry a contribution whose origins are exactly `[{\"component\": system}]`; there must be exactly one such contribution per equation and as many equations as `flatten` entries. Entry k's `species` is the species whose derivative the k-th equation's left-hand side takes (`D(x, t)`, or for a lifted equation the `faq` whose `expr` is `D(index(x, ...), t)`), and its `contribution` is that contribution's expression. When `lifted` is false the equation must also have exactly that one contribution, its right-hand side must equal `contribution`, and its left-hand side must be `D(species, t)`. Numbers compare by value and by kind: an integer literal in the corpus must be an integer literal in the binding (CONFORMANCE_SPEC §5.5.3.1 rule 3), so `-1.0` fails where `-1` is expected; TypeScript, whose numbers carry no kind, compares by value only.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "photostationary_loss_and_production",
      "description": "NO2 <-> NO + O3 with a parameter rate (R1) and an expression rate (R2). Every net coefficient is +1 or -1: a production term is the bare rate law, a loss term is `-1 * rate` with the INTEGER -1 (never a unary minus, never the float -1.0). Each species has two terms, so each right-hand side is one n-ary `+`. Species are declared NO2, NO, O3 -- not sorted.",
      "system": "Chem",
      "lifted": false,
      "derive_odes": [
        {
          "lhs": {"op": "D", "args": ["NO2"], "wrt": "t"},
          "rhs": {"op": "+", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": [-1, {"op": "*", "args": ["j", "NO2"]}]}, {"op": "*", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": [1.8e-12, {"op": "exp", "args": [{"op": "/", "args": [-1370, "T"]}]}]}, "NO", "O3"]}]}
        },
        {
          "lhs": {"op": "D", "args": ["NO"], "wrt": "t"},
          "rhs": {"op": "+", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": ["j", "NO2"]}, {"op": "*", "args": [-1, {"op": "*", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": [1.8e-12, {"op": "exp", "args": [{"op": "/", "args": [-1370, "T"]}]}]}, "NO", "O3"]}]}]}
        },
        {
          "lhs": {"op": "D", "args": ["O3"], "wrt": "t"},
          "rhs": {"op": "+", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": ["j", "NO2"]}, {"op": "*", "args": [-1, {"op": "*", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": [1.8e-12, {"op": "exp", "args": [{"op": "/", "args": [-1370, "T"]}]}]}, "NO", "O3"]}]}]}
        }
      ],
      "flatten": [
        {
          "species": "Chem.NO2",
          "contribution": {"op": "+", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": [-1, {"op": "*", "args": ["Chem.j", "Chem.NO2"]}]}, {"op": "*", "args": [{"op": "*", "args": [1.8e-12, {"op": "exp", "args": [{"op": "/", "args": [-1370, "Chem.T"]}]}]}, "Chem.NO", "Chem.O3"]}]}
        },
